    "title": "What is the WBC mercy rule for the 2026 World Baseball Classic? - Yahoo Sports",
    "text": "AdvertisementAdvertisementAdvertisementAdvertisementTRENDINGYankees shut out GiantsJudge's golden sombreroSign up for fantasy baseballOpening day starting pitchersSweet 16 previewStory byBryan Kalbrosky, For The WinFri, Mar 6, 2026·1 min readAdd Yahoo Sports on GoogleEspecially in the earlier phases of the World Baseball Classic tournament, not all of the games between nations are particularly evenly matched.Fans noticed that right away when Shohei Ohtani and Japan played against Chinese Taipei in the opening match. Ohtani hit a grand slam and Samurai Japan cruised on to victory, 13-0. The game, which was won by Dodgers star and 2025 World Series MVP pitcher Yoshinobu Yamamoto, was called after seven innings.AdvertisementAdvertisementAdvertisementThat is because a game is called if a team is winning by 15 or more runs after five innings and 10 or more runs after seven innings. Japan easily met that criteria with their 13-run lead at the end of the seventh.It is worth noting, though, that this rule is only in place during pool play and the quarterfinals.It does not count during the semifinals or the championship game, when a team would have the opportunity to come back from whatever deficit necessary.This article originally appeared on For The Win: What is the WBC mercy rule for the 2026 World Baseball Classic?AdvertisementAdvertisementTerms and Privacy PolicyYour Privacy ChoicesAbout Our AdsAdvertisementAdvertisementAdvertisement",
